    "teacher_markdown": "- Students choose their own story. Steer them toward low-stakes numbers they are comfortable sharing, and away from sleep, weight, grades, or money.\r\n- Numbers should be whole numbers from about 1 to 100, so the picture is easy to scale.\r\n- Hand trace example: numbers 20, 45, 30 give totals of 20, 65, and 95, and an average of about 31.7 for the three.\r\n- MY AI PROMPT: students complete the pattern. Check that the prompt says what `j` and `value` control, gives the biggest value, says what to draw with `average`, and tells AI Tutor to draw only, not to calculate the total or the average.- Students who cannot choose a story can pick from the idea list. A student's story can be silly, as long as it is a number they can count.\r\n- If a student's AI prompt is vague, ask three questions: What shape is each number? What does each variable control? What is the biggest value?\r\n- Students will see `main.py` and `story_review.txt` in their files. Both are for the next level, so ask students to work only in `story_plan.txt`.\r\n- This level has no Validate. The plan carries over to the next level in `story_plan.txt`.\r\n- Use this level to check whether students can plan a running total by hand, name the variables a program will use, and decide what to ask AI Tutor to draw and what to write themselves.",
